    {
      "id": "t006612",
      "source": "Problem 375. Pairwise collective choice: Sports Hall versus Park",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006613",
      "source": "Knowledge context.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006614",
      "source": "Group preferences can be aggregated in different ways. Pairwise majority asks a specific question about two options at a time.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006615",
      "source": "Given facts.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006616",
      "source": "Three public projects are considered: Park, Library, Sports Hall. Voter groups and rankings: 8 voters rank Park>Library>Sports Hall; 5 voters rank Library>Sports Hall>Park; 6 voters rank Sports Hall>Park>Library. Use pairwise majority: compare two options at a time; each voter supports the one ranked higher.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006617",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006618",
      "source": "For a pairwise contest, count how many voters prefer each of the two options. Ignore the third option for that comparison.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006619",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006620",
      "source": "In a pairwise vote between Sports Hall and Park, which option wins?",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006621",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006622",
      "source": "1. For each group, check whether Sports Hall or Park is ranked higher. 2. Totals: Sports Hall=11, Park=8. 3. Therefore the result is Sports Hall.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006623",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006624",
      "source": "Sports Hall wins 11 to 8.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006625",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006626",
      "source": "A collective decision rule must be explicit; different rules can summarize the same rankings differently.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006627",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006628",
      "source": "Preference rankings; for pair (a,b), sum weights of voters ranking a>b versus b>a.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006629",
      "source": "Part B — New and mixed reasoning domains",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006630",
      "source": "N1. Scale, proportion, and distance",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006631",
      "source": "Problem 376. Map scale 1:10000",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006632",
      "source": "Knowledge context.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006633",
      "source": "Map scale links a model to the real world through proportional reasoning.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006634",
      "source": "Given facts.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006635",
      "source": "A map has scale 1:10000. The measured map distance between two points is 4 cm. Rule: 1 cm on the map represents 10000 cm in reality; 100,000 cm = 1 km.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006636",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006637",
      "source": "Multiply map length by the scale denominator, then convert units. A scale is a ratio, not an added distance.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006638",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006639",
      "source": "What is the real straight-line distance in kilometres?",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006640",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006641",
      "source": "1. Real centimetres=4×10000=40000. 2. Convert to kilometres: 40000÷100,000=0.4.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006642",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006643",
      "source": "0.4 km.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006644",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006645",
      "source": "Scale problems are ratio problems: keep units explicit and conversion becomes mechanical.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006646",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t006647",
      "source": "Proportion d_real=d_map×denominator, followed by unit conversion.",
      "translation": ""
    },
